A function whose signature names `color` is promoted onto the package's struct
list by promoteForeignStructs, so SNGL's own `color` declaration reaches the
emitters beside the one each target carries for the type. Go answers with
snglcolor.Color and declares nothing; android declares the class itself, and
dedupes what it declares against the program's structs.

That dedupe matched the *source* spelling, `color`, against the emitted name,
`Color`, so it never fired: `MainScreen.kt` came out with two `data class
Color` -- the first from the SNGL declaration, whose uint8 channels are UByte,
the second the Int-channelled one every value and every Compose constructor
uses. Kotlin reports the redeclaration and then an argument mismatch per
channel per call, which is what a canvas full of colors makes of it.

The type annotation was the other half: IRTypeToKt answered `String` for a
color, so `bgOf`'s return type disagreed with the `Color(...)` it returns.

deny out/kotlin/android/MainScreen.kt `var r: UByte` -- a channel is Int, as it is in snglcolor, so the stdlib helpers and Compose's own constructor reach it without a per-channel conversion
deny out/kotlin/android/MainScreen.kt `: String = Color` -- a color-typed signature says Color

-- sig.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import draw "sngl:ui/draw"

output {
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

func bgOf(on bool) color {
    if on {
        return color.rgb(45, 255, 170)
    }
    return color.rgb(24, 60, 52)
}

window #w {
    var lit = false

    draw.canvas(width=100px, height=100px) {
        draw.rect(x=0.0, y=0.0, w=50.0, h=50.0, style=draw.Style{fill=bgOf(lit)})
    }
    button(text="toggle", @click { lit = !lit })
}
-- out/kotlin/android/MainScreen.kt --
